\begin{proof}
Assume that $G$ is simple. Since $|G|=1365=3\cdot5\cdot7\cdot13$, let $n_p$ denote the number of Sylow $p$-subgroups.

For $p=13$, Sylow's theorem gives $n_{13}\mid105$ and $n_{13}\equiv1\pmod{13}$. Thus $n_{13}\in\{1,105\}$, and simplicity implies $n_{13}=105$. Distinct subgroups of prime order intersect trivially, so these subgroups contribute $105(13-1)=1260$ distinct nonidentity elements.

For $p=7$, we have $n_7\mid195$ and $n_7\equiv1\pmod7$. The possibilities are $1$ and $15$, hence $n_7=15$, contributing $15(7-1)=90$ further elements. For $p=5$, $n_5\mid273$ and $n_5\equiv1\pmod5$, so $n_5=1$ or $21$; simplicity gives $n_5=21$, contributing $21(5-1)=84$ further elements.

Elements arising from Sylow subgroups for different primes have different orders, so they are all distinct. Consequently, $G$ has at least $1260+90+84=1434$ elements, contradicting $|G|=1365$. Therefore $G$ is not simple.
\end{proof}
